  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Thursday...\n\nWeak shortwave continues to produce showers as of noon, mainly\nSE of Billings, but we are starting to see development upstream\nover central MT. Air mass remains very moist with pwats ranging\nfrom an inch in our west to 1.5\" in the east. Dew points from\nLivingston to Harlowton are in the low 60s. Despite the transition\nto large scale subsidence behind the wave, there should be enough\nmoisture for isolated to scattered thunderstorms this afternoon\nand early evening. Mesoanalysis shows a surface trof from eastern\nMusselshell to Big Horn Counties, and convergence along this axis\nmay be a focus for thunderstorm development. Something to keep an\neye on. Any of today's storms could produce locally heavy rain and\nerratic winds. Isolated weaker convection may eke into our west in\nthe late evening but HRRR has backed off somewhat on this.\n\nBuilding ridge and shift to mixed west winds will push temps to \nthe upper 90s and lower 100s tomorrow. This marks the first of \nwhat looks to be several days of heat as the intermountain west \nridge remains in place through much of next week. Ensembles and \ncluster analyses show confidence here. Overall, look for high \ntemps from 95-105F each day through the extended period. As it \nlooks now, any break down of the ridge is expected to hold off \nuntil we get into August.\n\nRegarding the heat, extreme heat highlights are in effect tomorrow\nthrough the weekend. Along with the daytime temps will come warm\nnighttime lows, limiting relief. Please take precautions to \nprotect yourself from the heat.\n\nHumidity over the past 7-10 days has prevented fire dangers from \nelevating much. This is about to change. Today's wave will scour \nout the deep monsoon moisture and pwats will return to more \nseasonable levels starting Friday, though they will remain a \nlittle above normal for the next several days as we see occasional\npushes of mid-high level moisture from the south. Tomorrow's \nbreezy/mixed west winds will accompany RHs falling to the teens. \nThis will elevate concerns during what looks to be a transition/ \ndrying day for local fuels. Confidence has increased for a \nmonsoonal wave to bring scattered late day high-based \nthunderstorms Saturday. These storms won't be dry but precip will \nbe spotty. The combination of very hot temps, RHs in the teens, \nincreased lightning, and potential for strong convective surface \nwind gusts, warrants a Fire Weather Watch for Saturday from noon \nto midnight. Sunday will also be hot and dry but w/ lesser t-storm\npotential, and a weak backdoor wind shift. North winds may be \nsomewhat breezy in the east.\n\nAs already mentioned, the forecast for next Monday through\nThursday will be characterized by above normal temps and a daily\nrisk (10-25% chance) of diurnal thunderstorms. Pwats remain \nslightly wetter than normal so the storms won't be dry, but they \nwill be high-based and have potential to produce gusty/erratic \nsurface winds, which with the lightning itself will be problematic\nfor any new fires that develop over the coming days.\n\nFinally for those keeping up on the July 25th drama. Billings\nairport has never (since 1934) reached 100F on this date, but it\nreally should happen this year. Current forecast is for 102F,\nconsistent with 700mb temps of +16C and deep mixing. With the \nwater vapor transport and eventual convection on Saturday, it is \nat least plausible that cloud cover holds back surface heating \njust a bit. The July 25th record is 98F, so it is possible to \nbreak the record and still not reach 100F. Stay tuned.\n\nJKL",
